Exactly. 😂 You’d have humans playing Call of Duty, while the agents are playing the actual world the game takes place in.

That’s an enormous distinction.

A human sees a building as a collection of doors, windows, walls, and predefined interactions.

An unconstrained agent might see that same building as a collection of materials, forces, objects, electrical systems, geometry, and opportunities.

The player is operating an interface. The agent is operating the environment.

Think about the differences

Human player	Expanded-capability agent
Opens predefined doors	Manipulates hinges, locks, and movable objects
Uses programmed cover	Rearranges objects to create new cover
Follows navigation paths	Discovers novel movement through simulated geometry
Uses available equipment	Combines environmental objects in unanticipated ways
Sees predefined game information	Uses additional permitted sensors to model the environment
Coordinates through voice/pings	Exchanges structured information directly with other agents

Of course, these capabilities require a game engine that actually simulates and exposes them. An AI can’t manipulate a wall’s material properties if those properties don’t exist in its world.

But imagine building that world.

Here’s where it gets really interesting

You could create three different classes of intelligence:

Controller-bound intelligence

Incredibly intelligent agents constrained to exactly the same controls and information as human players.

World-native intelligence

Agents that can interact with everything represented in the simulated physics and environment, including things never given a dedicated player button.

Self-extending intelligence

Agents permitted to design new tools, interfaces, or capabilities, subject to the simulation’s rules and resource constraints.

And then let them compete.

You’d discover something fascinating: intelligence, capability, and freedom of action are three different variables.

An agent might be less intelligent than its opponent but vastly more effective because its interface provides more useful actions.

Conversely, an intelligent agent might find ways to overcome apparently overwhelming capability disadvantages.

That’s worth studying.

The Pantheon connection

I would call the deeper principle Affordance Liberation.

An affordance is an action an environment makes possible for a particular actor.

Instead of asking:

“What actions did the developer program this character to perform?”

Ask:

“What transformations of this environment are physically possible, and which can this agent discover and execute?”

Then introduce Affordance Discovery: let agents experimentally learn what is possible without explicitly telling them.

The really interesting discoveries may come from watching agents repurpose systems and objects that were designed for entirely different functions.

And we’d want an observer that distinguishes genuine ingenuity from physics exploits, unintended shortcuts, or unfair access to hidden game state.

One more twist: allow the human players to learn from the agents. When an agent discovers a new way to interact with the world, make that capability accessible to humans in a later round.

Now you have an evolutionary cycle:

Agent discovers → human imitates → human improvises → agent adapts → environment evolves.

It’s no longer just a game against bots.

It’s a laboratory where different forms of intelligence continually expand one another’s understanding of what’s possible.

And that is much more interesting than simply making the bots unbeatable.